P50, P95, and P99 are percentile metrics used to describe the distribution of response times (latency) in a system. They tell you what fraction of requests complete within a given time, instead of collapsing everything into a single “average.”  

What each percentile means

- P50 (50th percentile, median)  
  - Definition: 50% of requests are faster than or equal to this value; 50% are slower.  
  - Interpretation: “Typical” user experience.  
  - Example: If P50 latency = 80 ms, half of all requests finish in ≤ 80 ms. 

- P95 (95th percentile)  
  - Definition: 95% of requests are faster than or equal to this value; 5% are slower.  
  - Interpretation: “Most users, including many on slower paths.” Captures the tail of the distribution.  
  - Example: If P95 latency = 250 ms, 95% of requests finish in ≤ 250 ms; the slowest 5% take longer. 

- P99 (99th percentile)  
  - Definition: 99% of requests are faster than or equal to this value; 1% are slower.  
  - Interpretation: “Worst 1%” of requests – where you see rare but painful issues (GC pauses, lock contention, dependency timeouts, noisy neighbors).  
  - Example: If P99 latency = 600 ms, 99% of requests finish in ≤ 600 ms; 1% take longer. 

Why not just use average?

Averages are easily skewed by outliers. A few very slow requests can make the average look bad even if most users are fine, or hide serious tail problems if most requests are fast but some are extremely slow. Percentiles give a clearer picture of user experience across the distribution. 

Example:  
- 99 requests at 50 ms, 1 request at 5,000 ms  
  - Average ≈ 99.5 ms (looks “okay”)  
  - P50 ≈ 50 ms (typical user is fine)  
  - P99 = 5,000 ms (clearly shows a severe outlier problem) 

How they’re used in practice (SRE / backend / APIs)

- P50  
  - Baseline health / typical performance.  
  - Good for spotting broad regressions (e.g., after a deploy). 

- P95  
  - Common SLO target for “good performance for most users.”  
  - Used to tune system performance and catch moderate tail issues. 

- P99  
  - Critical for high-traffic or high-value paths (checkout, payments, key APIs).  
  - Exposes architectural bottlenecks and rare failure modes that P95 hides. 

In monitoring dashboards (Prometheus, Grafana, APM tools), you’ll often see latency plotted as P50/P95/P99 over time to understand both typical behavior and worst-case behavior under load. 

