Here’s how you can compute and use p50/p95/p99 latencies in Java, both from an in‑memory list and in a more realistic metrics setup.

1. From a `List<Long>` of latencies (ms)

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class LatencyPercentiles {

    public static record Percentiles(long p50, long p95, long p99) {}

    public static Percentiles compute(List<Long> latenciesMs) {
        if (latenciesMs == null || latenciesMs.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("latenciesMs must not be empty");
        }

        List<Long> sorted = new ArrayList<>(latenciesMs);
        Collections.sort(sorted);

        int n = sorted.size();
        long p50 = percentile(sorted, 0.50, n);
        long p95 = percentile(sorted, 0.95, n);
        long p99 = percentile(sorted, 0.99, n);

        return new Percentiles(p50, p95, p99);
    }

    private static long percentile(List<Long> sorted, double p, int n) {
        // Index for percentile p (0-based)
        int idx = (int) Math.ceil(p * n) - 1;
        idx = Math.max(0, Math.min(idx, n - 1));
        return sorted.get(idx);
    }

    // Example usage
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Long> latencies = List.of(
                10L, 12L, 15L, 20L, 25L, 30L, 40L, 50L, 80L, 120L,
                150L, 200L, 250L, 300L, 400L, 500L, 600L, 800L, 1000L, 1500L
        );

        Percentiles p = compute(latencies);
        System.out.println("p50 = " + p.p50() + " ms");
        System.out.println("p95 = " + p.p95() + " ms");
        System.out.println("p99 = " + p.p99() + " ms");
    }
}
```

This is fine for:
- Tests
- Small batch analyses
- Offline processing of logged latencies

For high‑throughput, concurrent services, use a proper metrics library.

