3. Using a `HdrHistogram` (high‑precision, low overhead)

For very high throughput and accurate tails, `HdrHistogram` is popular.

#Dependency

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.hdrhistogram</groupId>
    <artifactId>HdrHistogram</artifactId>
    <version>2.1.12</version> <!-- check latest -->
</dependency>
```

#Example

```java
import org.HdrHistogram.Histogram;

public class HdrExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Track latencies from 1 µs to 1 minute with 3 significant digits
        Histogram histogram = new Histogram(60_000_000L, 3);

        // Record some latencies in microseconds
        histogram.recordValue(10_000);   // 10 ms
        histogram.recordValue(20_000);   // 20 ms
        histogram.recordValue(50_000);   // 50 ms
        histogram.recordValue(100_000);  // 100 ms
        histogram.recordValue(500_000);  // 500 ms
        histogram.recordValue(1_000_000);// 1 s

        long p50 = histogram.getValueAtPercentile(50.0);
        long p95 = histogram.getValueAtPercentile(95.0);
        long p99 = histogram.getValueAtPercentile(99.0);

        System.out.println("p50 = " + p50 / 1_000.0 + " ms");
        System.out.println("p95 = " + p95 / 1_000.0 + " ms");
        System.out.println("p99 = " + p99 / 1_000.0 + " ms");
    }
}
```

Use this when:
- You have very high RPS
- You care about accurate tail (p99, p99.9)
- You want to aggregate histograms across time windows

